ple together uh  in places that are outside their comfort  zone uh  she said that has been uh helpful in  creating  some of these uh connections and holding  some of these difficult conversations  that i think people feel  inherently uncomfortable with and frank  what's next for you  on this journey i know you've got a lot  booked and and some of it is gonna kind  of  run alongside your column here but if  you could just give us a little sneak  peek about what you're  most excited about for the week to come  well  we had we had a great trip through  pennsylvania and i'll be writing about  that this week  um starting in philadelphia where there  are  there's a homeless encampment just  outside the philadelphia museum of art  which is the rocky steps and  the city is struggling with how to deal  with that i  had a great conversation um with  um with a guy who was the leader of a  band  many people on this stream probably know  called volktoni  they become a little bit of a streaming  streaming sensation but his songs have  um songs focus on  the underdogs and uh have socially  conscious bent to some of them and so we  talked a little bit about  uh what you know what what drives those  songs and  people's need for for music during all  of this uh  turmoil and sense of crisis so it's a  it's a little bit  uh tangential but i think still really  important so we're gonna be trying to  cover some of those  uh issues that uh or or aspects of life  that may not  be in the news every day but that i do  think are important to the way people  relate to  the way that they are experiencing life  and their communities  uh you've got a lot of ground to cover  frank so we don't take too much of your  time because you've got to get all the  way to san francisco  um but i'll remind our viewers that we  will be checking in with you every week  but before we let you go i just want to  know what it's been like in this tiny rv  with you and your wife as you're both  um working from home o